{
  "image_name": "636eb0671a0b636a37290572a7f04420.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how vertical land motion and surface elevation are measured relative to different Earth reference surfaces using ground surveying, GNSS, and satellite radar. It combines a coastal cross-section with measurement geometry to show that land subsidence is the downward movement of the ground surface relative to benchmarks, the geoid, ellipsoid, and ultimately sea level.\n\nIn the upper part, a levelling setup compares rod readings at two times to calculate a small elevation change, marked as subsidence, along a sloping land surface. This is set against broader geodetic surfaces such as topography, geoid, ellipsoid, sea surface, and global mean sea level, emphasizing that local ground lowering must be interpreted within these reference frames.\n\nThe lower part expands this into satellite-based methods. Several GNSS satellites transmit signals to an antenna fixed on the ground, allowing the antenna position to be solved in three dimensions over time with respect to Earth’s center of mass. Nearby, SAR interferometry is illustrated with two radar acquisitions; the phase difference between repeated observations gives a range change and thus surface displacement. Together, the figure shows complementary techniques for tracking coastal elevation change and subsidence from local benchmarks to global geodetic coordinates.",
